---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  DungeonIntro,
  Crossroads,
  DFSLocal,
  DFSGlobal,
  BFSLocal,
  BFSGlobal,
  Comparison,
  MapEditor,
} from "../../components/cave/dungeon-escape";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Escaping a Dungeon"
  description="Why breadth-first search (BFS) finds the shortest path and depth-first search (DFS) does not"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        One dungeon. Two algorithms. Depth-first search goes deep early,
        following one path. Breadth-first search moves step by step, exploring
        all nearby paths before going deeper.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you were trapped in this dungeon, and all you cared about was
        escaping through the shortest path... which one would you trust?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DungeonIntro client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's zoom into a single crossroads. From here, we can move in several
        directions.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Crossroads client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        DFS is a risk-taker. It commits to one path and follows it as far as it
        can. When that path leads nowhere, it backtracks to the last place with
        another option, and continues from there.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DFSLocal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        To make this work, we also need to remember where we've been. Otherwise,
        in a maze with loops, we could wander in circles forever.
      </p>
      <p>
        And one more detail matters: the path DFS finds can change depending on
        which direction it tries first.
      </p>
      <p>
        DFS hates hesitation. But that same strength can also be a weakness.
        Because it commits early, it may go deep into a bad branch before it
        even checks a better one nearby.
      </p>
      <p>
        So DFS can absolutely find an exit. But it doesn't guarantee the
        shortest one.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DFSGlobal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now look at BFS. Breadth-first search avoids committing too early. It
        explores the dungeon layer by layer. First, all positions one step away.
        Then all positions two steps away. Then three. And so on, until it finds
        the exit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead of diving into one corridor, it keeps a frontier. A whole edge
        of active possibilities. That is the price of being thorough.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <BFSLocal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        This is why BFS wins when the goal is the shortest path. It expands
        outward step by step from the start. That means the first path it finds
        to the destination is guaranteed to be the shortest.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <BFSGlobal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        But it pays for that guarantee. It has to keep the whole frontier in
        memory. That usually means more memory usage.
      </p>
      <p>
        DFS is lighter. It commits early, goes deep, and usually keeps less
        active information at the same time. So the real lesson is not that one
        algorithm is always better. The real lesson is that each one is good at
        a different thing.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Comparison client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        These are not the only search strategies. But breadth-first search and
        depth-first search are two of the most classic ones, and they already
        show a key trade-off between memory and optimality.
      </p>
      <p>
        So, if you were trapped in this dungeon, and all you cared about was
        escaping through the shortest path... BFS is your best friend.
      </p>
      <p>
        DFS also finds a way out. Sometimes even quickly. But it doesn't promies
        the best route.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <table
      style="width: 85%; margin: 0 auto; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 1.05em; text-align: center; zoom: 1.3;"
    >
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="padding: 0.5em 1em;"></th>
          <th
            style="padding: 0.5em 1em; color: #f59e0b; border-bottom: 2px solid #f59e0b; font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: 900;"
            >DFS</th
          >
          <th
            style="padding: 0.5em 1em; color: #3b82f6; border-bottom: 2px solid #3b82f6; font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: 900;"
            >BFS</th
          >
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Style</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;"
            >Goes deep first <span style="opacity: 0.45; font-size: 0.85em;"
              >(Commitment)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;"
            >Layer by layer <span style="opacity: 0.45; font-size: 0.85em;"
              >(Coverage)</span
            ></td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Memory</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;"
            >Usually <span style="color: #4ade80;">lower</span></td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;"
            >Usually <span style="color: #f87171;">higher</span></td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Shortest path</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 96 96"
              fill="none"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              style="display: inline-block; width: 1.4em; height: 1.4em; vertical-align: middle;"
            >
              <rect
                x="4"
                y="4"
                width="88"
                height="88"
                rx="18"
                fill="rgba(153,27,27,0.2)"></rect>
              <path
                d="M32 32 L64 64"
                stroke="#f87171"
                stroke-width="10"
                stroke-linecap="round"></path>
              <path
                d="M64 32 L32 64"
                stroke="#f87171"
                stroke-width="10"
                stroke-linecap="round"></path>
            </svg>
            <span style="opacity: 0.45; font-size: 0.8em; margin-left: 0.3em;"
              >(not guaranteed)</span
            >
          </td>
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 96 96"
              fill="none"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              style="display: inline-block; width: 1.4em; height: 1.4em; vertical-align: middle;"
            >
              <rect
                x="4"
                y="4"
                width="88"
                height="88"
                rx="18"
                fill="rgba(22,163,74,0.2)"></rect>
              <path
                d="M28 50 L42 64 L68 32"
                stroke="#4ade80"
                stroke-width="10"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
            </svg>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- Map Editor (helper slide for designing maps) -->
  <section data-visibility="hidden">
    <MapEditor client:only="react" />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
